---
import { elfEntryPath, systemVariablesPath } from '../data/manual-structure';
import { cuisPath } from '../data/cuis-calls';
import { pollPath } from '../data/polling';

interface Props { subject: string }
const { subject } = Astro.props;
---
{subject === 'subsystems' && <>
  <p>pTOS organizes its system interfaces around the TOS subsystems. GEMDOS provides file, memory and process services.
    BIOS handles basic device I/O and system vectors, and XBIOS provides extended hardware services.
    Each call’s reference describes the available targets and calling conventions.</p>
  <p>The graphical environment, GEM, uses AES for interaction with the user and VDI for drawing.
    CUIS provides shared window, input and session services to userspace <code>libaes.so</code> on x32.
    Line-A provides low-level 68k graphics operations, and XHDI defines a common interface to block storage drivers.</p>
  <p>Atari introduced TOS in 1985 with the ST and developed it further for the STE, Mega ST, TT and Falcon.
    Many pTOS extensions draw on later interfaces from FreeMiNT and MagiC. See
    <a href="/os-variants/">OS variants and extensions</a> for compatibility context.</p>
</>}
{subject === 'bios' && <>
  <p id="BIOS_2C_20The">pTOS uses the Basic Input/Output System (BIOS) for low-level character I/O, device status,
    disk sectors and system vectors. The target selector on each function page shows the calling convention.
    Device availability depends on the hardware and build.
    <a href="/subsystems/gemdos/">GEMDOS</a> provides higher-level file and device I/O.</p>
  <p>On protected builds, use the documented <a href={systemVariablesPath}>system variable access modes</a>.</p>
  <p>Under <a href="/os-variants/magic/">MagiC</a>, the BIOS is reentrant. An interrupt handler can call these functions
    when the supervisor stack has room. MagiC BIOS hooks should be reentrant and work independently of the contents
    of the legacy <code>saveptr_area</code>.</p>
</>}
{subject === 'xbios' && <>
  <p>pTOS uses the Extended BIOS (XBIOS) for hardware and firmware services such as video, audio, clocks and device control.
    Availability depends on the target and attached hardware. The function references describe the native bindings and
    calling requirements. Use <a href="/subsystems/gemdos/">GEMDOS</a> or <a href="/subsystems/bios/">BIOS</a>
    services where they cover the operation.</p>
  <p>Under <a href="/os-variants/magic/">MagiC</a>, the XBIOS is reentrant. An interrupt handler can call an XBIOS function
    when the supervisor stack has room.</p>
</>}
{subject === 'gemdos' && <>
  <p>GEMDOS provides the highest-level operating system functions in pTOS. It manages files, directories,
    memory and processes. On supported builds pTOS extends GEMDOS with native threads, process scheduling and
    per-process address spaces. The <a href="/subsystems/bios/">BIOS</a> and <a href="/subsystems/xbios/">XBIOS</a>
    provide the hardware-dependent services.</p>
  <p>GEMDOS function names usually start with a letter that identifies their category. <code>F</code> covers
    files and network calls, <code>D</code> directories, <code>P</code> processes, <code>M</code> memory,
    <code>S</code> system functions, <code>T</code> date and time, and <code>C</code> character input and output.</p>
  <p id="MiNT-compatible_20functions">pTOS implements a subset of the GEMDOS extensions introduced by FreeMiNT and
    <a href="/os-variants/magic/">MagiC</a>, adapting them for its native targets and memory model. For example,
    <a href="/reference/ssystem/">Ssystem</a> provides several FreeMiNT-compatible modes alongside pTOS-specific ones.
    Availability and behavior depend on the call, mode and build. MagiC 3.0 and some
    <a href="/historical/en/metados/">MetaDOS</a> drivers also implement some MiNT-compatible functions.</p>
  <p>GEMDOS is reentrant under MagiC, including its file system from MagiC 3.0.
    For example, moving a disk error alert may require allocating memory while a file operation is still in progress.
    MagiC can service that allocation during the file operation.</p>
</>}
{['aes', 'aes-fundamentals'].includes(subject) && <>
  <p>pTOS provides the classic kernel AES on supported flat-memory 68k and ARM builds. On x32 the experimental
    <code>libaes.so</code> runs in userspace and invokes <a href={cuisPath}>CUIS</a> system calls for shared window,
    input and session services.
    It supports multiple concurrent GEM applications and desk accessories. The available AES calls and extensions depend on the build.</p>
  {subject === 'aes-fundamentals' && <p>The application and accessory conventions below give the classic TOS,
    MiNT and MagiC context for these interfaces.</p>}
</>}
{subject === 'aes' && <>
  <p>Application Environment Services (AES) is the user interface layer of GEM. Applications use its libraries
    to manage windows, menus, dialogs and events. The <a href="/subsystems/vdi/">Virtual Device Interface</a>
    provides the graphics used to draw that interface. See the
    <a href="/conventions/programming-guidelines/style-guidelines/">GEM style guidelines</a> for application design.</p>
  <p id="AES_2C_20Origin_20of_20the">Digital Research developed GEM for MS-DOS PCs in 1984. AES was later
    ported to the Apple Lisa under CP/M-68K and to the Atari ST under GEMDOS. Other versions included GEM on X for Unix
    and X/GEM for FlexOS. AES 4.0 expanded the library interfaces.
    On implementations that provide <a href="/historical/en/appl-getinfo/">appl_getinfo</a>, applications can query
    supported options before using extensions.</p>
</>}
{subject === 'vdi' && <>
  <p>pTOS uses the Virtual Device Interface (VDI), GEM’s standardized graphics system, to provide device-independent
    drawing through workstations. Its function groups cover drawing, text, colour tables, raster operations and input.
    Applications can use the workstation’s capabilities to adapt to different devices and resolutions.</p>
  <p>The 68k and ARM ports use their platform-specific VDI bindings. On x32,
    builds with <code>CONF_WITH_X32_VDI</code> use a typed userspace client with operation-specific kernel services.
    The function references describe coverage for each target. <a href="/subsystems/aes/">AES</a> uses VDI to draw its interface.</p>
  <p>pTOS builds with scalable-font support include a built-in GDOS for those fonts. On classic TOS, additional fonts and
    output devices could require a disk-loaded GDOS such as FSM-GDOS or SpeedoGDOS. NVDI also provided a replacement VDI. See
    <a href="/os-variants/ptos/fonts/">Font installation</a> for build and target requirements.</p>
</>}
{subject === 'line-a' && <p>On pTOS, the classic Line-A interface is available on 68k builds that enable
  <code>CONF_WITH_LINEA</code>. For graphics across architectures, see <a href="/subsystems/vdi/">VDI</a>.</p>}
{subject === 'xhdi' && <p>pTOS builds with <code>CONF_WITH_XHDI</code> expose the XHDI cookie and entry point for
  block devices. The function references describe the built-in operations and the limits of the classic interface.</p>}
{subject === 'process-functions' && <p>pTOS builds with native scheduling support process and thread creation
  through <code>Pclone</code>, plus process waits and executable replacement. On eligible MMU
  builds, <code>Pfork</code> and <code>Pvfork</code> create private copy-on-write
  children. FreeMiNT’s <code>Pvfork</code> allows the child to share ordinary memory with the parent.</p>}
{subject === 'file-functions' && <>
  <p>On managed pTOS builds, threads in one process use the same descriptor table. Duplicated or inherited descriptors
    have separate close rights and share the underlying open file and its current position. Separate opens of a regular file
    have independent positions. Readiness waits keep track of the original descriptor binding, so closing and reusing its
    number does not redirect a pending wait. <a href={pollPath}>Fpoll_time64</a></p>
  <p>Use <code>F_GETFL</code> to read a native descriptor’s open access mode. <a href="/historical/en/fcntl/#ptos-file-notes">Fcntl</a></p>
</>}
{subject === 'support-for-alternative-file-systems' && <>
  <p>pTOS provides GEMDOS file and directory services on its built-in FAT filesystem. Eligible ARM and 68k builds can
    enable <code>CONF_WITH_PLUGGABLE_FS</code>, allowing an integrated provider such as virtio-9p to expose a GEMDOS drive.
    A provider can supply a readiness callback for its open files. Polling a provider file without that callback
    reports <code>POLLERR</code>. <a href={pollPath}>Fpoll_time64</a></p>
  <p>The FreeMiNT <code>XFS</code> driver interface and calls such as <code>Dopendir</code> and <code>Dreaddir</code> are outside
    the current pTOS implementation. The interfaces below describe the options introduced by MiNT and other TOS variants.</p>
</>}
{subject === 'gemdos-error-messages' && <p>pTOS GEMDOS reports errors as negative return values. Use each function’s
  return-value documentation for its pTOS behavior. The table includes codes defined by other TOS variants.</p>}
{subject === 'signals' && <p>pTOS provides native process and thread control through the
  <a href="/subsystems/gemdos/process-functions/">GEMDOS process functions</a>. The MiNT signal handling interfaces
  described below are currently unimplemented in pTOS.</p>}
{subject === 'pipes-test-for' && <p>pTOS currently leaves <a href="/historical/en/fpipe/">Fpipe</a> unimplemented.
  The checks below apply to TOS-compatible systems that expose pipes on drive <code>U:</code>.</p>}
{subject === 'network' && <p>pTOS currently leaves the MiNTnet socket calls unimplemented. This section documents
  the BSD socket and vectored I/O calls in the MiNTnet ABI.</p>}
{subject === 'argv-procedure' && <p>Classic PRG startup and ParaTOS guest launches use the ARGV environment convention.
  Native ELF applications launched with <a href="/reference/pexecve/">Pexecve</a> or
  <a href="/reference/shel-writeve/">shel_writeve</a> receive explicit vectors through the <a href={elfEntryPath}>ELF32 initial stack</a>.
  libcmini translates legacy ARM and x32 <code>Pexec()</code> calls to those vectors.</p>}
{subject === 'shell-library' && <p>On pTOS x32, use <a href="/reference/shel-writeve/">shel_writeve</a> to
  launch from GEM with explicit vectors and <a href="/reference/shel-readve/">shel_readve</a> to inspect the
  application’s startup snapshot. Both <a href="/historical/en/shel-write/#ptos-launch">shel_write</a> and
  <code>shel_writeve</code> can select ELF accessory startup.</p>}
{subject === 'os-variants' && <p>The pTOS chapter covers configuration, applications and
  startup conventions. The other chapters describe TOS variants and environments whose interfaces provide
  compatibility context for pTOS development.</p>}
{subject === 'make-up-of-the-magx-inf-file' && <p>pTOS boot settings are documented under
  <a href="/os-variants/ptos/ptos-ini/">PTOS.INI</a>. <code>MAGX.INF</code> configures MagiC.</p>}
{subject === 'threads-in-magic' && <p>For native threads in pTOS, see <a href="/reference/pclone/">Pclone</a> and
  <a href="/reference/pthjoin/">Pthjoin</a>. The conventions below describe the MagiC thread API.</p>}
{subject === 'shared-libraries' && <p>pTOS uses a userspace linker for <a href={elfEntryPath}>ELF32 shared libraries</a>
  on supported configurations. The format below describes MagiC’s shared libraries.</p>}
{subject === 'tos-list' && <p>See <a href="/os-variants/ptos/">pTOS</a> for its current development configuration
  and startup conventions. This table records releases from the TOS family.</p>}
{subject === 'three-important-programming-rules' && <p>On pTOS, use the system interface for each service and
  its documented target binding. In particular, applications on protected builds should read system variables
  through <a href={systemVariablesPath}>the supported Ssystem modes</a>.</p>}
{subject === 'olga-protocol' && <p>On pTOS, AES provides application messaging on builds that include an AES.
  OLGA also requires participating applications and an OLGA manager. Compatibility with those components on
  current pTOS builds is unverified.</p>}
{subject === 'ptos' && <p>pTOS is an EmuTOS-derived operating system with native builds for 68k, ARM and x32.
  Its boot files depend on the target and build configuration. These sections cover executable handlers,
  external EmuDesk and EmuCON, fonts, and the startup ABI for native ELF programs.</p>}
